Two of the priorities in prehospital care are to identify time sensitive processes (they need to get to the hospital quickly) and to identify reversible processes (you can do something in the field to fix or significantly help the problem). List 2 things that could cause altered mental status that are either time sensitive OR reversible (they do not need to be applicable to this patient).

An example of a reversible cause of altered mental status is high altitude cerebral edema.  If it is identified in the field the reversal is to bring the patient down from elevation as quickly as possible.   

1. Hypoglycemia: A low blood glucose level can quickly affect brain function and cause confusion, agitation, seizures, or loss of consciousness. I would check the patient's blood glucose level as soon as possible. If hypoglycemia is confirmed, treatment would depend on her ability to swallow safely and the applicable EMS protocols.

2. Opioid overdose: Opioid toxicity can cause altered consciousness, slow or absent breathing, and potentially fatal respiratory depression. This is a time-sensitive emergency because inadequate ventilation can lead to hypoxia and cardiac arrest. I would support airway and breathing and administer naloxone when indicated and authorized by protocol.

