Prompt 10
 The patient is already on the gurney/ambulance bed. What are 2 strategies for managing the violent or combative patient that you learned in this course?

1. Verbal de-escalation and reassurance

I would approach the patient calmly, speak in a quiet and respectful voice, and explain what is happening before attempting any procedures. I would avoid arguing, making threats, or surrounding her with too many people. I would give her simple choices when appropriate and allow her time to respond. My goal would be to reduce her fear and agitation so that she feels safer and becomes more willing to cooperate with treatment.

2. Safe use of restraints when necessary

If the patient remained physically aggressive and posed an immediate danger to herself or others, I would coordinate with the ALS team and follow local EMS protocols for physical restraints. Restraints would be used only when necessary, with appropriately trained personnel, and would never be used as punishment. I would avoid positioning that restricts breathing, particularly prone restraint. After restraint, I would continuously monitor her airway, breathing, circulation, mental status, and the condition of her restrained extremities. Any medication used for severe agitation would require appropriate authorization and qualified personnel.

